+## Subtopics
+
+### Opacity-Gradient Pruning
+A complementary strategy that removes Gaussians with low opacity or high opacity gradients after densification. It targets redundant primitives and works synergistically with LAG to further reduce Gaussian count [S1, unofficial]. See [Soft Pruning](5590-soft-pruning.md) for related pruning techniques.
+
+### CUDA Backward Optimization
+An optimization technique that reduces the computational cost of the backward pass, often used in conjunction with LAG to further improve training efficiency [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Adds minimal overhead per iteration (computing binary mask and accumulating history). Densification step itself is not more expensive than standard densification, but the total training time is reduced due to fewer Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing $K$ binary masks of size $W \times H$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:**
-    *   On Mip-NeRF 360 bicycle scene: Reduced peak Gaussians from 947,136 to 547,083, training time from unspecified to 190.0s, SSIM from 0.751 to 0.734, PSNR from 25.153 to 24.939 [S1, unofficial].
-    *   On Mip-NeRF 360 (aggregate): Reduced NGS from 0.77M to 0.47M, training time from 245.7s to 215.4s [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Combined with CUDA optimizations and pruning: LAG+Prune achieved 0.41M Gaussians on Mip-NeRF 360, compared to 0.47M (LAG) and 0.63M (Prune) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces redundant Gaussian growth, shortens training time, reduces memory footprint [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Slight decrease in rendering accuracy (SSIM/PSNR). Requires tuning of $\tau$ and $ds\_threshold$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When training time and memory are constraints, and a slight drop in rendering quality is acceptable. Particularly effective when combined with pruning [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When maximum rendering quality is required and computational resources are not a bottleneck [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The additional cost of LAG is the computation of the loss map and mask, which is done during the standard backward pass. The DS update requires summing mask values for covered pixels, which is proportional to the number of pixels covered by each Gaussian. This is generally low overhead compared to the rasterization and rendering steps [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the loss map, mask history (K iterations), and DS buffer. Memory usage is $O(W \cdot H \cdot K + N)$, where $W, H$ are image dimensions, $K$ is the accumulation window, and $N$ is the number of Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On the Mip-NeRF 360 bicycle scene, LAG reduced the peak number of Gaussians from 947,136 to 547,083 and training time from unspecified to 190.0 seconds, with SSIM dropping from 0.751 to 0.734 and PSNR from 25.153 to 24.939 [S1, unofficial]. Across Mip-NeRF 360, LAG reduces NGS from 0.77M to 0.47M and training time from 245.7s to 215.4s compared to the Base configuration [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** LAG scales with the number of Gaussians and image resolution. The mask accumulation is independent of $N$, but the DS update depends on coverage.
+*   **Strengths:** Reduces redundant Gaussian growth, shortens training time, and produces more compact scene representations [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Slight decrease in rendering metrics (SSIM/PSNR) due to suppressed densification in well-reconstructed areas [S1, unofficial]. Requires careful tuning of $\tau$ and $K$.
+*   **When to Use:** When training time and memory are constrained, and a slight reduction in peak rendering quality is acceptable for a more compact model. Particularly effective when combined with pruning [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When maximum possible rendering quality is required, or when the scene has dynamic regions where reconstruction quality changes rapidly and $K$ cannot adapt quickly enough.
 
 ## Background
-LAG Densification was proposed in the paper "Loss-Guided Density Control and Efficient Backward Optimisation for Compact Gaussian Splatting" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the inefficiency of standard 3DGS adaptive density control, which can introduce many redundant primitives, especially in high-frequency texture regions where gradients are large [S1, unofficial]. The method leverages the existing loss map from the backward pass, avoiding additional rendering passes or external estimations [S1, unofficial].
+Loss Accumulation Guided (LAG) densification was proposed in the paper "Loss-Guided Density Control and Efficient Backward Optimisation for Compact Gaussian Splatting" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the issue of excessive Gaussian proliferation in standard 3D Gaussian Splatting, where positional gradients can be large in high-frequency texture regions, leading to unnecessary Gaussian growth and increased computational load [S1, unofficial]. The method leverages the loss maps generated during standard backpropagation to identify persistently under-reconstructed image regions, providing a more stable and reliable measure of reconstruction quality than gradients alone [S1, unofficial].
 
